    <meta name="citation_reference" content="Jones, C. W. et al. Molecular and physiologic changes in the SpaceX Inspiration4 civilian crew. Nature 
                  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07648-x
                  
                 (2024). The SpaceX I4 mission, which included the first all-civilian crew and innovative in-flight experimental technologies, demonstrated that short-duration spaceflight induces temporary physiological and stress responses, supporting the likely safety of such missions and establishing a valuable biomedical database for future astronaut health countermeasures."/>
    <meta name="citation_reference" content="Overbey, E. G. et al. The Space Omics and Medical Atlas (SOMA) and international astronaut biobank. Nature 
                  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07639-y
                  
                 (2024). Spaceflight missions offer opportunities to study multi-omic and physiological changes in astronauts, as detailed in SOMA, which presents comprehensive multi-omic samples and data from the SpaceX I4 crew, available through public and controlled-access biobanks and data repositories to accelerate biomedical discoveries for long-duration space missions."/>
